# Naming

Only `ID`, `HP`, `NPC`, `URL`, `UTC`. Not HTTP, JSON, or units: casing there is meaning (`Mb` ≠ `MB`, `Ms` ≠ `MS`).

Never `Id`, `Hp`, `Npc`, `Url`, `Utc`. PascalCase: `NPCDirector`. camelCase: `npcID`, `fromURL`. Whole name: `id`, `hp`, `npc`, `url`, `utc`.

Timestamps: game time fields/variables are named `timestamp` or `*Timestamp`. UTC fields/variables end with `UTC` (`savedUTC`). Never `At`.

No leading or trailing `_` on names (`no-underscore-dangle`). Privacy is the `private` keyword, not a prefix.

**File names** (TypeScript). Name a file as C# or C++ would. A file whose content is one class or one type, with at most what belongs to it, is PascalCase and named after it, exactly (`Persona.ts`, `SceneRTS.ts`, `NPCInput.ts`). Every other file is kebab-case: lowercase words joined by `-`, acronyms as plain lowercase words, no `_` (`sight.ts`, `game-verbs.ts`, `npc-input.ts`). A file that mixes functions, constants, types or classes is kebab-case, even when it centers on one class; it does too much, and splits later. Folders are lowercase, kebab-case when more than one word (`dungeon-vault/`). No two files share a name anywhere in the repo, ignoring case and `-`, except `index.ts` and `index.html`; `pnpm lint` checks this and the casing. Config files are exempt from every naming rule: `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `.gitignore`, `vite.config.ts`, `eslint.config.ts` and the like. A test is its subject's name plus `.test` (`Persona.test.ts`). When a name is taken, use the class it holds, or a folder or purpose word in front (`rts-types.ts`, `watch-score.ts`). A host class that would share a library class's name takes the `Demo` prefix; the library keeps the plain name.
